  "live_records": {
    "q-108970": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108970",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X∼N(5,10)$ and $Y∼N(6,11).$ Find the distribution of the random variable $W=2X-5Y.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2})$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=2X-5Y & ∼N(2\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 6,{2}^{2}\\cdot 10+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 11) \\\\  & ∼N(-20,315).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $W∼N(-20,315).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
