    "q-175410": {
      "dom_id": "step-q175410",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If ${X}_{1}∼N(-3,1)$ and ${X}_{2}∼N(1,2)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2} & ∼N(1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 1,{1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 2) \\\\  & ∼N(0,19).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y∼N(0,19).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-175410-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "Not enough information to determine the answer",
              "html": "Not enough information to determine the answer"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-175410-b",
